[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Installation, CLI Setup, and PostCSS Configuration in Tailwind CSS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covers the foundational steps required to set up Tailwind CSS in a modern web development project. You will learn how to install the necessary packages, initialize the configuration, set up the Tailwind CLI, and configure PostCSS for optimal asset compilation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Installing Tailwind CSS via npm"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open your terminal and navigate to your project directory. Run the following command to install tailwindcss, postcss, and autoprefixer as development dependencies:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"npm install -D tailwindcss postcss autoprefixer"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Generating Configuration Files"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, generate your configuration files for both Tailwind and PostCSS. You can use the Tailwind CLI to initialize a basic tailwind.config.js file:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"npx tailwindcss init -p"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command creates two files in your root directory: tailwind.config.js and postcss.config.js."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Configuring Template Paths"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open your tailwind.config.js file and configure the "content" section with the paths to all of your template files so Tailwind can tree-shake unused styles in production:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  content: ["./src/**/*.{html,js,jsx,ts,tsx}"],
  theme: {
    extend: {},
  },
  plugins: [],
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Setting Up PostCSS"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Verify that your postcss.config.js file is properly configured to include tailwindcss and autoprefixer plugins. It should look like this:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  plugins: {
    tailwindcss: {},
    autoprefixer: {},
  },
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 5: Adding Tailwind Directives to CSS"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a main CSS file (for example, src/input.css) and add the @tailwind directives for each of Tailwind's layers:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 6: Running the Tailwind CLI Build Process"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Finally, use the Tailwind CLI to scan your CSS file for directives and compile your output CSS file. Run the CLI compiler in watch mode during development:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"npx tailwindcss -i ./src/input.css -o ./src/output.css --watch"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure your HTML file links to the generated output.css file rather than the input file."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The --watch flag ensures automatic recompilation whenever you modify your source code or configuration."}]
